    "A molecule's overall polarity depends on both its bond polarities and its shape. Even if every bond is polar, the bond dipoles can cancel in a symmetric molecule.",
    "Polarity also explains why some gases trap heat in the atmosphere. A molecule absorbs infrared light only if its vibration changes the molecule's dipole moment. N<sub>2</sub> and O<sub>2</sub>, which make up 99% of air, cannot do this. CO<sub>2</sub> has no permanent dipole, but when it bends or when one C=O bond stretches while the other shrinks, a temporary dipole appears, so CO<sub>2</sub> absorbs infrared light and acts as a greenhouse gas.",
